diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — Campus 1 (172.16.10–30.x hosts) unable to reach Campus 2 (10.5.x and 10.6.x hosts) — cannot be confirmed by any live evidence or historical trace in the current lab state.

**Affected component:** None currently. Every subsystem examined is healthy.

**Mechanism (absent):** The inter-campus forwarding path runs Campus 1 hosts → mswitch0 (VLAN10/20/30 gateway) → router16 (bond0, 10.2.2.x) → router7 (172.30.1.x) → router8 (172.25.1.x) → router17 (172.29.10.x) → sshswitch (55.5.1.x) → Campus 2 hosts. All OSPF adjacencies along this chain are Full and every router carries 18 consistent OSPF routes, including the Campus 2 prefixes 10.5.0.0/24 and 10.6.0.0/24 advertised by sshswitch.

**Localization:** Not applicable. Targeted pings from pc5 (Campus 1, VLAN10) to pc1 (10.5.0.1), pc2 (10.5.0.2), and dc_pc5 (10.5.0.5) all returned 0% loss at ~2 ms. The Phase 1 reachability sweep confirmed 0% loss from every Campus 1 host to 10.6.0.4. Campus 2 → Campus 1 is equally clean.

**Root cause:** No anomaly detected. All carrier counters on inter-campus router uplinks (router16, router7, router17, router8) show exactly 2 transitions — the Kathara container startup baseline — ruling out post-startup link flaps. FRR logs are empty (no recorded OSPF events), and no flap or stress processes were found anywhere. The user's reported past outage leaves no mechanism or trace in the live lab.

**Fix:** No corrective action required. If the issue recurs, capture a real-time reachability snapshot at the moment of failure to identify the affected path.
